<!-- Website teaching layer for kotlin/type-system/generics.md. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A crash report says <code>ClassCastException: LinkedTreeMap cannot be cast to User</code>, thrown from <code>users.first().name</code> on the profile screen. The variable is declared <code>List&lt;User&gt;</code>. The parser ran successfully two screens earlier. "This can't happen" — except it did.</p>

Generics give the compiler a precise picture — `List<User>`, not just "a list" — and it checks every use against that picture. But on the JVM most of the picture is **erased** before the program runs: at run time a list is just a list. This chapter separates what is checked at compile time from what still exists at run time, shows the tools for crossing that gap (`reified`, type tokens, variance), and explains the crash above step by step.

<!-- @after First Principles :: visual -->
### Two pictures of the same list

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-cols" style="--cols:2"><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Compile time — the compiler's picture</span><div class="node is-key"><strong><code>val users: List&lt;User&gt;</code></strong><small>every <code>add</code>, read and call is checked against <code>User</code></small></div><div class="node"><strong><code>users.first().name</code></strong><small>allowed: the element type is known to be <code>User</code></small></div></div><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Run time — what the JVM keeps</span><div class="node"><strong>a <code>java.util.List</code></strong><small>the <code>&lt;User&gt;</code> is gone: erased</small></div><div class="node" data-state="warn"><strong>a cast to <code>User</code>, inserted where an element is read</strong><small>the only run-time trace of the type argument</small></div></div></div><figcaption>Because the compiler trusts its picture, a value that sneaks in through an unchecked path is not caught when it enters — only when someone reads it as the promised type.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Type Erasure — The Core Constraint :: animation -->
### The opening crash, step by step

<figure class="stepper" data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">How a <code>List&lt;User&gt;</code> ends up full of maps</span><div class="stage viz-stack"><div class="node" data-el="parse"><strong><code>gson.fromJson(json, List::class.java)</code></strong><small>the parser is told only "a List" — no element type</small></div><div class="down" data-el="d1"></div><div class="node" data-el="maps"><strong>Elements are built as <code>LinkedTreeMap</code></strong><small>without a type, Gson makes generic key–value maps</small></div><div class="down" data-el="d2"></div><div class="node" data-el="assign"><strong><code>val users = … as List&lt;User&gt;</code></strong><small>an unchecked cast: erasure means nothing can be verified here</small></div><div class="down" data-el="d3"></div><div class="node" data-el="read"><strong><code>users.first().name</code> — two screens later</strong><small data-text="readNote">the inserted cast to User runs now</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
{"label":"Parse","caption":"The call passes List::class.java. Because of erasure there is no Class object for List<User>, so the element type is simply missing.","add":["parse"],"state":{"parse":"warn"}},
{"label":"Build","caption":"Gson cannot know the elements should be User, so it builds each one as a LinkedTreeMap.","add":["d1","maps"],"state":{"maps":"warn","parse":""}},
{"label":"Assign","caption":"The result is cast to List<User>. At run time that cast only checks \"is it a List?\" — the compiler warns it is unchecked, and the code moves on.","add":["d2","assign"],"state":{"assign":"warn","maps":""}},
{"label":"Read","caption":"Far away, reading an element as User triggers the cast the compiler inserted. The element is a map, so it throws ClassCastException — at the reader, not the parser.","add":["d3","read"],"state":{"read":"error","assign":""}},
{"label":"Fix","caption":"Give the parser the full type: a type token (Moshi's Types.newParameterizedType, Gson's TypeToken) or a serializer that knows List<User> (kotlinx.serialization). The failure, if any, then happens while parsing.","state":{"read":"done","parse":"done","maps":"done","assign":"done"},"text":{"readNote":"elements are real User objects"}}
]</script><figcaption>Erased type arguments make unchecked casts look successful. Every <code>@Suppress("UNCHECKED_CAST")</code> deserves a reason why this cannot happen.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after reified — Escaping Erasure with inline :: visual -->
### What `reified` changes at each call site

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-pair"><div class="pane"><span class="pane-title">You write</span><pre>inline fun &lt;reified A : Activity&gt;
    Context.start() =
  startActivity(Intent(this, <mark>A::class.java</mark>))

context.start&lt;DetailActivity&gt;()
context.start&lt;SettingsActivity&gt;()</pre></div><div class="pane"><span class="pane-title">Each call site receives (conceptually)</span><pre>startActivity(Intent(context,
  <mark>DetailActivity::class.java</mark>))

startActivity(Intent(context,
  <mark>SettingsActivity::class.java</mark>))</pre></div></div><figcaption>Because the body is copied into each caller, the compiler can write the concrete class there. That is why <code>reified</code> requires <code>inline</code> — and why it still cannot recover a nested argument such as the <code>User</code> in <code>List&lt;User&gt;</code> for APIs that take a plain <code>Class</code>.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Variance — in, out, and Why List<Dog> Isn't List<Animal> :: visual -->
### Why the compiler refuses `val animals: MutableList<Animal> = dogs`

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-flow"><div class="node"><strong><code>dogs: MutableList&lt;Dog&gt;</code></strong><small>holds only dogs</small></div><div class="arrow"></div><div class="node" data-state="warn"><strong>alias it as <code>MutableList&lt;Animal&gt;</code></strong><small>(if this compiled)</small></div><div class="arrow"></div><div class="node" data-state="error"><strong><code>animals.add(Cat())</code></strong><small>legal for a list of animals</small></div><div class="arrow"></div><div class="node" data-state="error"><strong><code>dogs[0].bark()</code></strong><small>the "dog" is a cat → crash</small></div></div><figcaption>Reading is safe in this direction, writing is not. <code>out</code> promises "only read", which is exactly what makes <code>List&lt;out Animal&gt;</code> accept a list of dogs. The next chapter, Variance, builds this into a full model.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Type Tokens — The Reflection Workaround :: check -->
**Check yourself.**

<details><summary>A generic class needs <code>T::class.java</code> for logging, but it is not an inline function. What do you do?</summary>

Pass the class in: `class Store<T : Any>(private val type: Class<T>)` (or `KClass<T>`). Only inline functions with `reified` parameters can produce it from `T` alone.
</details>

<details><summary>Why does <code>if (x is List&lt;String&gt;)</code> fail to compile, while <code>if (x is List&lt;*&gt;)</code> works?</summary>

At run time only "is it a List?" can be checked — the `String` argument was erased. `List<*>` asks exactly the question the JVM can answer; checking the elements needs a separate pass over them.
</details>

<details><summary>Your repository returns <code>Resource&lt;Nothing&gt;</code> for <code>Loading</code>. Why can it be assigned to <code>Resource&lt;User&gt;</code>?</summary>

`Resource` is declared `out T`, and `Nothing` is a subtype of every type, so `Resource<Nothing>` is a subtype of `Resource<User>` — and of every other `Resource<…>`.
</details>
